feat: validation, data layer, next-occurrence, export and import

Tasks 9, 11, 12 and 17. Everything but the screens.

The parity suite immediately earned itself. Zod accepted an `at` schedule
carrying `intervalMinutes` while Postgres refused it on `schedule_shape`,
because Zod strips unknown keys by default — exactly the asymmetric drift
the suite exists to catch, found on its first run. Fixed by declaring the
other shape's fields as `z.undefined().optional()`, which mirrors the
CHECK precisely while staying tolerant of unrelated keys, since the same
schema validates imported files.

`nextOccurrence` in packages/shared duplicates the SQL deliberately: the
activities list shows "next at 07:00" per row and the alternative is a
round trip per render. Duplication is only survivable if something holds
the copies together, so occurrenceParity.test.ts drives eight fixtures
through both implementations in two zones — including both 2026 London
daylight-saving transitions — and asserts identical instants. Nairobi is
in there precisely because it never changes its clocks, so agreement
cannot be an accident of both being wrong the same way.

The TypeScript side finds the instant for a wall-clock time by probing
and correcting rather than by offset arithmetic, because across a DST
change the offset the answer needs is not the offset the guess had. Two
passes settle it.

describeWeekdays and WEEKDAY_LABELS moved to packages/shared. Two copies
would drift in the worst way available: the UI promising "Weekdays" while
the scheduler queues Sunday.

Water completions do not go to activity_log. They write a water_entries
row through the existing water feature, because water already has a goal,
a daily total and a UI built on that table — two records of how much
someone drank would surface as the goal not moving when they log a glass
from a notification.

Export and import came forward from task 17 because `.default([])` makes
the parsed type require the new keys, so typecheck demanded it. The keys
are optional on the way in: every export already downloaded has no
`activities` key, and rejecting those files would make this a breaking
change for exactly the users careful enough to keep a backup. Tested
first and explicitly.

import_fastr_export is replaced wholesale rather than gaining a sibling,
because the point of it is that an import is one transaction — a second
call could succeed after the fasts failed. Activities are inserted in a
loop since each schedule needs the id of the row just created, and the
export carries no database ids at all, which is what lets a file be
imported into a different account. The 20-activity limit still applies
and fails the whole file: silently dropping the twenty-first would leave
the user with no way to know what was lost.

fastr_user_row_counts had to be dropped rather than replaced — `create or
replace view` cannot insert columns mid-list and fails with "cannot
change name of view column". The new counts belong beside the other user
tables, not tacked on after `profiles`, because that view is how account
deletion is proved and a table missing from it is a cascade nobody
checked.

682 tests pass.
